  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nRest of Today into Tonight... After seeing the first freeze of \nthe season for the core Twin Cities metro this morning we will \nsee a gradual warming period. Despite being much colder today \nthan what we have seen recently, this is actual right about \nnormal for this time of year. The normal high for today at MSP \nis 47 and the normal now is 32, so only a few degrees off of \nwhat we are seeing today. Tonight will be another cooler night \nand will depend on how the clouds evolve. Fairly confidence we \nwill see clouds spread across western Minnesota helping limit \nradiative cooling. To the east though this cloud cover gets less\ncertain and we will see more radiative cooling and there looks \nto be a push of some CAA across Wisconsin. So it will be this\narea with the coldest temperatures tonight.\n\nSaturday into next week... Overall this looks like a fairly dry\nperiod with the only chance in the main deterministic global\nmodels being a possible Alberta Clipper late Wed into Thursday.\nNot seeing much in the NBM right now through, as this is not as\nconsistently shown within the ensemble solutions. This keeps \nthe NBM based long term forecast dry. We had such a wet start \nto the month though that we are at 800 percent or higher month \nto date on precipitation at our STC and MSP climate sites. \nTo put this another way, we would still be above normal if we \nsaw no additional rain or snow at our STC and MSP climate \nstations for the rest of the month. Past the dry part of the \nforecast the other key message will be a return to above normal\ntemperatures. After Friday's taste of what normal November \ntemperatures are like we will warm back to above normal. Mostly \nneutral to warm air advection through mid week before the \nclipper chance. After that we could see another cooler, closer \nto normal, period late next week. However another rebound still \nlooks likely so this next round of cold air does not look like \nit will be the one the makes to cold last. Looking into longer \nrange model guidance it might not be until late November or even\nearly December before we get some more consistent cold to stick\naround. Put another way, it is going to be a while before we \nstart seeing lake ice or a snowpack. Which was expected for a\nstrong El Nino year.",
